dialogue cohesion_redmage_knight
title: Warm iron
trigger: Cohesion redmage knight level=2
once
speaker commander $commander
speaker redmage redmage
speaker knight knight

node open commander
  After the ford the Knight sits with the shield across both knees, working the strap buckle with fingers gone white and slow. The Red Mage watches from across the fire and has already pulled one glove off.
  -> fingers

node fingers redmage
  You've been at that buckle since the water. Your fingers aren't working, Knight. Give them here. I can have them warm before the kettle is.
  -> holds

node holds knight
  In the holds, you warm your own hands or you lose them. Nobody puts heat into you. It goes in one door and out another, and then there's two cold people.
  -> heat

node heat redmage
  I know how heat goes in and doesn't stay. I did that for a whole winter. This isn't that. This is a buckle and a pair of hands. Commander, tell the Knight to hold still.
  * Hold your hands out, Knight. The Red Mage knows exactly how much. -> hands
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 10
    do: AddTrust knight -3
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:warm_hands
  * Warm the shield, not the Knight. Iron keeps heat longer than skin. -> iron
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 6
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:warm_iron
  * The Knight's fine. Red Mage, keep your fire for the field. -> field
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight -4
    do: AddTrust redmage -2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:warm_field
  * We said fire would be tried together, never alone. Try it here, with the Knight. -> together
    if: FlagSet redmage:test_promised
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 8
    do: AddTrust redmage 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:warm_together

node hands knight
  Fine. Fine. There. That's, huh. That's not a fire at all. That's like a hearth through a wall. You've done this before, Red Mage.
  -> tin

node iron redmage
  Iron, then. Hold the rim. Not the face, I won't touch the old line. There. Now when you grip, it gives the heat back slowly.
  -> tin

node field knight
  The field. Right. Thank you anyway, Red Mage. I'll sit closer to the kettle. That much I can manage myself.
  -> tin

node together redmage
  Together. Then you tell me when it's enough, Knight. That's the part I never had. Someone telling me when to stop.
  -> tin

node tin redmage
  While I've got you. When we fight, I'm the one nearest whatever's burning, and the tin rides on my coat. If I go down, it goes in the mud. You stand behind that shield all day. Would you carry it?
  * Beside, not in front. The tin rides between the two of you. -> beside
    if: FlagSet knight:line_beside
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 10
    do: AddTrust knight 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:tin_beside
  * It's going home to the aunt's hearth. Knight, see it gets there whatever happens to the rest of us. -> home
    if: FlagSet redmage:fire_home
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 8
    do: AddTrust redmage 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:tin_home
  * The Knight carries it. Nothing gets past that shield. -> carries
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 8
    do: AddTrust redmage -2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:tin_carried
  * It stays on the Red Mage. Some things you don't hand over. -> stays
    do: AddCohesion redmage knight 2
    do: SetFlag redmage_knight:tin_stays

node beside knight
  Between us. I like that better than in front. Strap it to my belt on your side, Red Mage, and stay where you can reach it.
  -> close

node home knight
  To the hearth. I've carried worse promises over worse ground. If the tin and I get there alone, I'll knock and say who sent me.
  -> close

node carries redmage
  Behind the shield. It's never been out of my reach in nine years. I'll hate every minute. Don't drop it, Knight. Don't you dare.
  -> close

node stays knight
  Then I'll stand where the tin is. Same thing, from my side. Keep your coat buttoned, Red Mage.
  -> close

node close commander
  The buckle comes loose on the next try. The Knight sets the shield down on the Red Mage's side of the fire, face up, close enough to catch the warmth.
